 ## Background
-The concept of projecting tetrahedra for volume rendering was introduced by Kindlmann et al. in 1998 [S1, S2, unofficial][S4, S6, S7][S8, S9, S10, unofficial][S11][S12, unofficial][S14, S16][S17, S18, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of ray casting on unstructured grids, offering a way to utilize standard graphics hardware for interpolation and compositing [S2, unofficial][S4, S6, S7][S12, unofficial][S14, S16]. The method has since evolved to include parallel sorting strategies [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial], bandwidth-optimized primitives [S1, S2, unofficial], and stochastic rendering variants [S1, unofficial].
+The use of tetrahedra in volume rendering originated from the need to handle unstructured grids and resolve topological ambiguities in isosurface extraction. The Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm was developed to overcome the ambiguities of Marching Cubes by ensuring consistent topology [S1, unofficial]. Projected Tetrahedra was introduced as an object-order rendering technique to leverage hardware rasterization for unstructured data [S1, S2, unofficial][S3][S4, S6, S7][S8, S9, S10, unofficial][S11][S12, unofficial][S14, S16][S17, S18, unofficial]. Parallel methods using kd-trees and grid-based traversal were later developed to handle large-scale datasets and improve performance [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S8767]. The technique is used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and differentiable rendering frameworks [S1, unofficial][S4].
 
